  "answer": "### Short Answer\nAn individual is required to file an annual income tax return if their taxable income exceeds the threshold set by the tax rates, which is currently P250,000 for the taxable year.\n\n### Controlling Authorities\nThe requirement for filing an annual income tax return is governed by Section 24 of the National Internal Revenue Code (NIRC), which outlines the income tax rates applicable to individuals.\n\n### Interpretation\nUnder Section 24 of the NIRC, individuals who are citizens or resident aliens of the Philippines are subject to income tax on their taxable income derived from all sources within and outside the Philippines. The tax rates are structured progressively, with individuals earning not over P250,000 being exempt from income tax. Those with income exceeding this threshold must file a return and pay taxes according to the applicable rates.\n\n### Practical Meaning\nIn practical terms, individuals earning an annual income of P250,000 or less are not required to file an income tax return, while those earning above this amount must file their returns and pay the corresponding taxes based on the graduated tax rates. This requirement ensures compliance with tax obligations and contributes to the government's revenue collection.",
